# TODO - Universal build command

## Objective

Expose one build command accepting a title and optional target.

## Acceptance

- Select only the required modules and features.
- Use the host native toolchain for native desktop packages.

## Evidence

The host-side `games-build` crate selects the MAME source family, machine
and revision for asset verification, conversion, publication and read-back.
Its dependency on native storage excludes runtime TLS and game logic.
The `build TITLE [linux-x86-64]` command selects the authored title
package, binary and native-preview feature; no unrelated workspace packages
are requested. It invokes the host Cargo toolchain with the locked release
profile and explicit GNU/Linux x86-64 target. Unknown titles, targets and
additional arguments are rejected. The actual build and hidden native smoke
succeeded for `simpsons-arcade`. Android and browser compilation, portable
packaging and automatic asset discovery remain unimplemented.

Acceptance review, 2026-11-25: the title selector admits exactly the
current authored title, its package, binary, required runtime feature
and explicit native target. An omitted target selects the existing
Linux x86-64 release path. The toolchain IR owns host selection and
compilation; the portable build command does not invoke a native SDK
or select an OS itself. The independent all-feature graphs reject
source decoders in runtimes and authenticated LAN in the asset build.
Synthetic controls reject unknown titles, targets and additional flags;
local release builds and package executions are recorded in native and
release-checks.

The universal selected-title command is established for the supported
native target. Further title registrations, Android and Web linking,
packaging, portable deployment and physical-device acceptance remain
separate tasks. Unsupported targets return explicit errors.
